# 106 · A rig on Apple silicon that uses its GPU

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.3.0](../roadmap.md#v030) · **Tracked in:** [GitHub issue 87](https://github.com/iammrduncan/thermite/issues/87), the source of truth for this work. This file keeps its context.

Built on 2026-11-13 and proven on one Mac. It waits for the owner's hands-on look.

## What

A rig on a Mac with Apple silicon uses the Mac's GPU. The rig stays what a rig is, a harness container and its supervisor with the same wall, and the GPU reaches the harness as a Vulkan device.

- **How the GPU gets there.** Containers on macOS run in a Linux virtual machine. One kind gives its containers the Mac's GPU: a Podman machine made with the libkrun provider. The container sees `/dev/dri`; Mesa's Venus driver in the container carries Vulkan to the Mac, where it becomes Metal.
- **What it serves.** Engines with a Vulkan back end. It does not serve MLX, which needs Metal: that is a runner on macOS itself, goal [279](279-runner-on-macos-for-mlx.goal).
- **What was built,** each part in Progress:
  1. A target, `vulkan-gpu`, beside `cpu` and `cuda-gpu`.
  2. The harness on a Vulkan base, and the rig's device setup, fence and isolation check for a GPU that is `/dev/dri`.
  3. The one-machine install on a Mac: `thermite doctor` and `thermite local init` say whether the Mac's machine gives its GPU, and `local init` writes the compose file that gives it to the rig.
  4. A smoke kit for the target, which the judge's smoke check runs.

The goal as first written (2026-10-23) planned a rig on macOS itself, without containers, for MLX. The study of 2026-11-13 found the way through Vulkan, which keeps the rig's wall as it is, and the owner chose it. The rig on macOS itself is goal 279 now.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-23, on the one-machine install: "If they have just mac it will pick up the unified memory and the apple silicon GPU for running mlx style stuff etc..." For v0.1.0 the install said plainly that a Mac's rig gets the virtual machine's CPUs and memory and no Apple GPU ([037](_completed/037-gpu-compose.goal)).

On 2026-11-13 the owner wanted a second, local-only Thermite on a Mac for trying v0.3.0, with the Mac's own GPU for small models.

## Done when

Written by the builder on 2026-11-13, when the build began.

- [x] **The target.** A rig with `THERMITE_TARGET=vulkan-gpu` names its device (a name, a driver, a UUID, an id that stays the same), and is ready for timing only when Vulkan lists a real GPU. A rig that sees only the software device, or none, says so in words and measures nothing.
- [x] **The wall holds.** On a rig with a Vulkan GPU, the roles that must not use the GPU can't open its device nodes, and the isolation check proves it for each role and each node, on the real machine.
- [x] **The install.** On a Mac, `thermite doctor` and `thermite local init` say whether Podman's machine gives the GPU, and what to do when it doesn't. When it does, `local init` writes `compose.gpu.yaml`, and the stack started with it has a rig that reports the GPU to control.
- [x] **The judge measures on it.** The smoke check of that rig, asked through control, passes with the Vulkan smoke kit: each candidate gets its expected verdict from the real judge, on the Mac's GPU.
- [x] **The pages and the credits** say what exists.
- [ ] **The owner has tried it,** hands-on.

Not in this goal:

- MLX and Metal work, which need a runner on macOS itself: goal [279](279-runner-on-macos-for-mlx.goal).
- A released Vulkan harness image. The image builds from the source: goal [280](280-released-vulkan-harness-image.goal).
- A limit on the GPU's memory. The owner, 2026-11-13: none is needed (Decisions).
- A rig with a Vulkan GPU set up by control on a machine or a cluster, more than one Vulkan GPU in a rig, and a development GPU of the agents' own. A Mac has one GPU and runs the one-machine install.
- Other GPUs through Vulkan on Linux (AMD's, Intel's). The target's code does not name Apple, but nothing else is tested. AMD GPUs are goal [105](105-amd-gpus.goal).

## Decisions

- 2026-11-13, the owner, asked what GPU the local-only Thermite on a Mac should use for trying v0.3.0: "use this local mac. For tests we can do very small models. we should make an mlx, metal capable rig image." So this goal was wanted, for small models first. No step of [v0.3.0, step by step](../v030_release_plan.md) holds it: it was built beside the steps, at the owner's command of the same day.
- 2026-11-13, the owner, later the same day: "figure out how to get my mac to have 10gb of its GPU as a runner... there has to be a way to do this, someone has figured it out... check out apple/containers." The study of that day is in Progress.
- The builder's note on "image", of that day, before the study: an image alone can't give a rig the Mac's GPU, since a container on macOS runs in a Linux virtual machine that can't reach Metal. The study then found the one kind of machine that carries Vulkan to it.
- 2026-11-13, the owner, asked whether to run the proof (krunkit and its parts from Homebrew, a second Podman machine, one small model): "Run the proof now".
- 2026-11-13, the owner, when the build began: "just do one libkrun podman machine and run both control and runner containers on it etc... its fine we can use that qwen model and it will never use all my ram." So:
  - **One machine.** Control, the bus and the rig run in the one libkrun machine. Thermite makes no machine of its own: the machine is the Mac owner's, and `local init` and `thermite doctor` read it.
  - **No limit on the GPU's memory.** The "10 GB" of the earlier words is not built. The Vulkan device gives no figure to hold a limit to, and the owner's test models are small.
- The builder's choices, 2026-11-13. Each can be changed:
  - **The way through Vulkan,** and MLX apart (goal 279), as the study's "What fits Thermite as it is" says.
  - **The target is named for what it reaches through, `vulkan-gpu`,** not for Apple: its driver reads any Vulkan device and refuses the software one. Only a Mac's GPU is tested.
  - **The image is built on a distribution's own Mesa,** Ubuntu 26.10's 26.2, pinned by digest. The proof used the machine's makers' patched Mesa; the build then found that Mesa 26.2 and newer open the GPU as they come, and older ones do not (Progress). No third party's package source is in the image.
  - **The fence alone keeps roles off the GPU.** In a rootless container on the machine the broker can't give the device nodes a group, so the layout is the shared one with the Landlock fence, which a rig with NVIDIA GPUs already has for that case. The fence got one new rule, for `/dev/dri`.
  - **The judge can't ask whether the GPU is quiet.** The device reports no load, memory in use or temperature. The target is ready when the GPU is listed; a disturbed measurement shows in the run's own repeats and its noise figure.
  - **On a Mac where Docker and Podman both answer, Podman is the rig's engine** when its machine gives the GPU, unless `--engine` names another.

## Open questions

- Whether a run on this GPU needs a memory budget of its own later, for larger models: the device reports the Mac's whole memory as its own, and the Mac's other work shares it.
- Whether the same target serves AMD and Intel GPUs on Linux, whose Mesa drivers the image also carries. Not tested; goal [105](105-amd-gpus.goal).
- Whether the checks can cover this path without a Mac. Today they hold the parts (the driver on real `vulkaninfo` text, the device nodes and the fence's rule, the compose file, the kit's shape); the GPU itself needs a libkrun machine, which no CI runner has.

Settled on 2026-11-13: a container with Vulkan through the virtual machine was worth the look, and it is what was built; the wall on macOS itself, and whether that is one piece of work with goal [277](277-runner-without-a-container-engine.goal), moved to goal 279.

## Progress

- 2026-10-23: Goal written as a plan, from the owner's words.
- 2026-11-13: **A study of how others give contained work a Mac's GPU.** Read from public pages only; nothing was installed or run, and nothing is built. The works are in [CREDITS.md](../../../CREDITS.md).

  | Way | What the contained work gets | Where it stands |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | Apple's `container` tool and its Containerization framework | No GPU. Linux guests only | A maintainer, June 2025: "we do not currently support this". No release up to 1.7.1 names a GPU |
  | Podman with a libkrun machine (krunkit) | Vulkan, for compute only, in a Linux container given `/dev/dri`: Mesa's Venus driver in the container, virglrenderer and MoltenVK on the Mac, then Metal | Works today. Its makers measured llama.cpp's Vulkan back end in a container at 74 to 80% of Metal's speed on an M4 Pro (June 2025). The container needs their patched Mesa |
  | llama.cpp's GGML-VirtGPU back end over the same machine | llama.cpp alone, near Metal's speed: its calls go straight to the Mac's Metal back end | In llama.cpp since January 2026. It needs a virglrenderer with patches not yet in its upstream, and its makers say the first versions are not safe for more than one tenant |
  | A program on the Mac that holds the GPU, which containers ask | Whatever that program offers: text generation from a model, in the ones read (Docker Model Runner; an MLX toolkit for Apple's `container`) | Works, but the work on the GPU is the program's, outside the wall. A kit's own engine does not run there |
  | MLX in a Linux container | Nothing: MLX needs Metal, which no Linux guest has | Not possible. MLX runs on macOS itself |

  - **What fits Thermite as it is:** the second way. A runner stays what it is, a harness container and its supervisor, with the same wall and the same isolation self-test, and it is given the Mac's GPU as a Vulkan device. It needs a target class for a Vulkan GPU beside `cuda-gpu` and `cpu`, a harness image with the Vulkan parts, and a kit whose engine has a Vulkan back end. It does not give MLX.
  - **What MLX needs:** a runner on macOS itself, as What says. That is the larger piece, with the wall as its open question, and is one question with goal 277.
  - **"10 GB of its GPU":** no page read gives a setting that caps the GPU's memory for a libkrun guest. Its makers state one limit: the guest's memory and the GPU's together are at most 64 GB. So the cap would be the runner's own: the target says how much it may use, and refuses a model and context that need more. To be measured in a proof.
  - **A limit found:** Podman runs one machine at a time, and a machine's provider is chosen when it is made. So a local Thermite and its GPU runner live in one libkrun machine, and a machine of the other provider can't run beside it.
- 2026-11-13: **The proof, on one Mac, at the owner's yes** ("Run the proof now"). An M4 Pro with 24 GB, macOS 26.5, Podman 5.8.2 from Homebrew. Nothing of Thermite's code changed.
  - **Set up:** krunkit 1.3.2 with libkrun 1.19.8 and MoltenVK 1.4.2, from the libkrun makers' Homebrew tap; a second Podman machine made with the libkrun provider (6 CPUs, 10 GiB); a Fedora 44 image with the makers' patched Mesa (25.3.6) and llama.cpp built in it with its Vulkan back end.
  - **The GPU is there:** the machine has `/dev/dri`, and a container given `--device /dev/dri` lists `Virtio-GPU Venus (Apple M4 Pro)`, Vulkan 1.2, driver `venus`.
  - **Speed,** one 0.5B model (Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, Q4_K_M, 463 MiB), llama.cpp at one commit, `llama-bench` with 512 prompt tokens and 128 generated, 3 runs each:

    | Where | Prompt, tokens a second | Generation, tokens a second |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | On macOS itself, Metal | 7,117 | 276 |
    | In the container, the GPU through Vulkan | 5,378 | 213 |
    | In the container, no GPU given, 6 CPUs | 531 | 232 |

    The container's GPU runs at 76% of Metal for the prompt and 77% for generation, as its makers measured. A model this small generates as fast on the CPU; the GPU's gain shows in the prompt, ten times the CPU's.
  - **Memory:** the Vulkan device reports one heap of 24 GiB, the Mac's whole memory, whatever the machine's own size (10 GiB here), and gives no figures of what is in use. So nothing below holds the work to 10 GB: the runner must keep the budget itself, by what it loads.
  - **One machine at a time, as read:** the default machine had to stop while the libkrun one ran, and the local Thermite with it. Its containers did not come back by themselves when the default machine started again; `compose up -d` brought them back.
  - **What was left to build then,** for a runner that uses a Mac's GPU this way: a target for a Vulkan GPU, a harness image with the Vulkan parts and the rig's GPU setup for `/dev/dri`, the one-machine install on a Mac, and a smoke kit; MLX apart. The next entry is that build.
- 2026-11-13: **Built, and proven on the same Mac.** The same M4 Pro, with one Podman machine on libkrun (8 CPUs, 12 GiB) holding control, the bus and the rig.
  - **The target, `vulkan-gpu`** ([`vulkan.ts`](../../../src/harness/targets/vulkan.ts)). It reads the devices from `vulkaninfo --summary`, run as the role that may open the GPU. Its identity is the device's name, driver and UUID, with an id made from the name and Vulkan's vendor and device numbers. It takes the first real GPU and never the software device (Mesa's llvmpipe, which computes on the CPU): with only that one, or none, the rig is not ready and says why. The rig's hardware report shows the device; its memory, bus and limits are empty, because the device reports none.
  - **A Mesa that opens the GPU as it comes.** The proof's image had the machine's makers' patched Mesa. Tried on the same machine, each distribution's own Mesa:

    | Base | Mesa | The GPU |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | Debian 13 | 25.0.7 | Not opened: `ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY` |
    | Ubuntu 25.10 | 25.2.8 | Not opened, the same |
    | Ubuntu 26.04 | 26.0.8 | Not opened, the same |
    | Debian testing | 26.1.6 | Not opened, the same |
    | Ubuntu 26.10 | 26.2.4 | Opened |
    | Fedora 44 | 26.2.6 | Opened |

    So the harness's Vulkan base is Ubuntu 26.10, pinned by digest: the stage `vulkan-base` of [`containers/Containerfile`](../../../containers/Containerfile), with Mesa's Vulkan drivers, the Vulkan loader, `vulkaninfo`, the headers and `glslc`. The harness is the same `harness` target, built on that stage.
  - **The wall.** The rig's GPU device nodes are `/dev/dri/renderD*` and `/dev/dri/card*` on this target ([`devices.ts`](../../../src/harness/broker/devices.ts)). The broker starts every role without the GPU behind the fence's new `--no-dri` ([`devfence.c`](../../../containers/harness/devfence.c)), and the isolation check tries each node as each role. On the Mac, asked through control: "isolation holds: every probe of 10 roles came out as it must (devices: shared (fence: landlock))", with `/dev/dri/card0` and `/dev/dri/renderD128` refused (`EACCES`) to each role that must not have them.
  - **The install** ([`machine.ts`](../../../src/cli/machine.ts)). On a Mac with Apple silicon and Podman, `thermite doctor` and `thermite local init` ask Podman for its machines and look for `/dev/dri` in the one that runs. Each case has its sentence and its fix: no machine running, a machine of another provider, a libkrun machine with no GPU device. With the GPU there, `local init` writes `compose.gpu.yaml`: the harness on the Vulkan base, `THERMITE_TARGET=vulkan-gpu`, and `/dev/dri`. A checkout's file builds the image; a release's must be told one (goal 280).
  - **The smoke kit** ([`kits/smoke-vulkan`](../../../kits/smoke-vulkan/kit.json), with [`examples/smoke-vulkan-project`](../../../examples/smoke-vulkan-project/README.md)): a matrix product in a Vulkan compute shader, as `smoke-cuda`'s is in CUDA, with the same four candidates and a proof that the reference was not called.
  - **The judge's smoke check on the Mac's GPU,** asked through the local control, passed, every candidate as expected:

    | Candidate | What it is | Verdict |
    | --- | --- | --- |
    | The baseline | | 582 GFLOP/s on both cells (small ±0.16%, large ±1.94%) |
    | A/A | The baseline against itself | -0.05% [-1.20%, 1.11%], block sigma 1.4% |
    | `noop` | A renamed variable | `neutral` |
    | `broken` | Half of the sum skipped | `invalid`: "max abs error 10.31 above 0.002" |
    | `fallback` | Calls the reference | `invalid`: "dispatch proof failed: reference op called 120 times" |
    | `good` | A 4 by 4 block of outputs for each thread | `win_screened`, then `win_confirmed`, +228% |

  - **The local-only Thermite** now runs this way: its rig reports its hardware to control as "shared: Virtio-GPU Venus (Apple M4 Pro)", with the device's UUID.
  - **What the checks hold, and what they can't.** Tests hold the driver on real `vulkaninfo` text (the Mac's device, the software device, none), the device nodes and the fence's argument for each role, the hardware report, each answer of Podman about its machine, the compose file `local init` writes, and that the kit is whole. No check reaches a real GPU through Vulkan: that needs a libkrun machine, so the isolation check and the smoke check above were run by hand, and `--no-dri` was tried by hand in the image.
  - **Changed on the Mac, outside Thermite:** Homebrew installed krunkit, libkrun and MoltenVK; Podman's provider there is libkrun now, and its machine of the default provider is stopped and kept.
  - **Checks run:** the whole test suite (3,044 of 3,048 side by side with 4 skipped, 214 of 215 alone with 1 skipped, none failed), the types, the lint, the public, size, import and loose-ends checks, the image pins, and the documentation's build and check. The compose form of the Mac's file for a release was resolved by compose both ways: with the image named, and without, where compose stops with the file's own words. Not run: the heavy acceptance checks. None of them reaches this target, and the path they share (the CPU and CUDA rigs) is held by the tests.
- 2026-11-13: **The pushed build, a8436656, on the local-only Thermite.** The stack was rebuilt from the branch as pushed, with `compose.gpu.yaml`. The rig reports the GPU, the isolation check holds with the same words, and the judge's smoke check passed again in 13 minutes: "smoke on smoke-vulkan: all 4 candidates were judged as expected".
- **For the owner's look:** the rig's page on the local-only Thermite (its hardware, and its **Isolation** and **Judge smoke** checks), `npx thermite doctor` on the Mac, the page [Install on one machine](../../_site/src/content/docs/how-to/install/one-machine.md) under "Use a Mac's GPU", and the choices in Decisions.

